mike - a friendly log viewer
============================

mike is a terminal viewer for streaming logs. point it at a command
(or pipe logs into it) and it gives you live tailing, native JSON
parsing, search, and field picking. no query language to learn.

USAGE

    mike <command> [args...]      run a command, watch its logs
    <command> 2>&1 | mike         pipe logs in
    mike --demo                   play with pretend logs

when mike runs the command itself it captures stdout and stderr
separately; stderr lines get a red mark in the gutter.

KEYS

    /        search. plain words match anywhere in the line.
             field=value matches a JSON field. numbers compare
             numerically: latency_ms>200, code>=500, n<10.
             terms are ANDed: service=api latency_ms>200 timeout
    k        field picker. tick the JSON fields you want with
             space, enter when done. nothing ticked = show all.
    up/down  select a line
    enter    expand the selected line into pretty JSON
    y        copy the selected line (raw) to the clipboard
    e        show errors only (press again to turn off)
    w        show warnings and errors
    j        expand every line into pretty JSON
    f        pause/resume following
    r        restart the command (not in pipe mode)
    c        clear the screen
    g / G    jump to top / bottom
    esc      clear selection, then search, then level filter
    q        quit

NOTES

    lines that parse as a JSON object get colour by level, with
    time/level/message fields shown first. everything else passes
    through untouched.

    search applies live as you type. matches are highlighted.

    while scrolled up the header counts new matching lines
    arriving below.

    memory is capped at 50000 lines (-max-lines to change).

BUILD

    go build -o mike .

requires go 1.25 or newer.
